  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\n   The central idea of this approach is to use a multi-resolution optimization strategy that combines coarse-.\n   grid combinatorial optimization with periodic structure exploitation. This plan addresses the key limitations of the previous solution by focusing on:\n\n   - Maintaining periodic structure throughout the optimization process to facilitate the discovery of globally periodic solutions\n   - Using a block structure for the step function rather than a fine-grained representation\n   - Incorporating multiple phases with increasing resolution\n   - Employing both binary and continuous optimization representations\n\n   We suspect this approach can significantly improve upon the parent solution by leveraging periodic patterns to reduce worst-case overlaps. The previous solution specifically limited the maximum overlap to 0.38086 and we aim to get below 0.38080.\n\n   The essential limitations are:\n   - Computation is currently bench-marked at a single resolution\n   - The periodic structure may introduce artifacts if not properly \n   optimized \n   - The block-count selection requires careful calibration\n\n   2. Mathematical Specification\n   - Representation: Define h(x) over [0,2] as a periodic function with period P samples. The domain is represented by n_points equally spaced points, and P is chosen as a divisor of n_points.\n   - Variables:\n     * h_periodic[0..P-1]: The fundamental block of the periodic function\n     * P: Period length in samples\n     * n_blocks = ceil(n_points / P): Number of full periods in the grid\n     * dx = 2.0 / n_points: Grid spacing\n   - Objective: C_5 = max_k { \u222b_{0}^{2} h(x) (1-h(x+k)) dx } computed as np.correlate(h,1-h,'full') * dx\n   - Constraints:\n     * 0 \u2264 h_periodic[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n     * \u2211_{i=0}^{P-1} h_periodic[i] = P/2 (per the sum constraint)\n       * This ensures the total integrated value is dx * (sum_period) * ceil(n_points / P) = 1\n\n   3. Implementation Procedure\n   We use a multi-phase approach with the following stages:\n\n   Phase 0: Initialization\n   - If parent provided: Extract the first P samples\n   - Else: Generate random block satisfying sum constraints\n\n   Phase 1: Coarse-Grid Period Selection\n   - Search period P over a grid of divisors of n_points (1,2,4,5,10...)\n   - For each P, compute n_blocks and the total grid size N = P * n_blocks + P (to complete periods)\n   - Low-cost combinatorial optimization (with 0/1 values) to identify promising P\n\n   Phase 2: Continuous Optimization with Periodicity\n   - Fix the best P from phase 1\n   - Use L-BFGS-B on h_periodic with:\n     * Linear constraint: np.sum(h_periodic) - P/2 = 0\n     * Objective: C_5 computed for the periodic grid\n\n   Phase 3: Upsampling and Final Optimization\n   - Increase resolution if time allows\n   - Binary polishing followed by smooth optimization\n\n   4. Search Choices and Variations\n   Default period search: [1,2,4,5,10,25,50] (will expand if time allows)\n   Smooth opt: alpha=10.0, mu=1.0 unless performance stalls (then increase by factor 2)\n   Initial period search: 10 periods, only 3 candidate P values\n   If initial block fails, try binary structure: np.where(h_periodic > 0.5, 1, 0)\n\n   5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n   Estimate phases:\n   - Phase 0: ~10 seconds\n   - Phase 1: ~30-100 seconds (10 period candidates)\n   - Phase 2: 400-600 seconds (main opt)\n   If resolution increases: Double time for grid doubling\n   Fallback: Use parent's solution and targeted refinements\n\n   6. Validation and Return Contract\n   - Inputs: h_values must:\n     * Sum of elements: approximately n_points/2 in value (only 500 samples)\n     * All elements in [0,1]\n   - Outputs:\n     * h_values: Vector meeting the above constraints\n     * c5_bound: Maximum correlation value\n     * n_points: Final grid resolution\n\n   This plan intentionally omits global optimization to focus on proven methodology. Let's proceed.",
